<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.T3.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">PetaFLOPs for the distillation of Qwen-1.5-1.8B (<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="3.2.1 Benchmarking with Qwen ‣ 3.2 Pretraining Distillation from Scratch ‣ 3 Experimental Details ‣ Don’t Ignore the Tail: Decoupling top-𝐾 Probabilities for Efficient Language Model Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>) on a subset of 1M tokens from the Regmix dataset. TAD has a similar PFLOP to Vanilla KD, while MiniPLM is higher than both. The methods involving sequence generation (SeqKD or MiniLLM) are too expensive to scale to billions of tokens.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
